project_objective:
Leveraging successes to date, acknowledging areas for improvement and understanding the critical need for efforts to be driven forward, iMMAP plans to continue to provide support to all Syria crisis response actors (from cross-border hub and from inside Syria). As such, it involves support to the to the coordination and common services sector partners: OCHA, the cluster/sector leadership, its members, NGO fora, and UN agencies through the following information management (IM) themes. 

Specific objectives:
1.	Strengthening the IM knowledge, skills and experience of information and data management staff through our capacity strengthening programs; 
2.	Making IM expertise available to humanitarian actors to standardize practices (principles, tools, processes, skills) through our common services program;
3.	Enhancing coordination mechanisms and providing direct technical support to actors and initiatives;
4.	Improving food security analysis inside Syria whilst taking into consideration the surrounding countries affected by the crisis; and
5.	In collaboration with partners, gain a better understanding of the urban context inside Syria.

A  travers  ce  nouveau  projet  COOPI  se  propose  de  continuer  à renforcer les actions passes et surtout le renforcement des capacités des RECOPE, d’AFASVER et des affaires sociales qui garantiront la pérennisation de la réponse à la fin de ce projet.COOPI  continuera aussi à  donner  un appui à la mise en réseau  et coordination  des  tous  les  acteurs  internationales  et  locaux  dans  la zone.En général, on mettra en œuvre des interventions de:
1)  renforcement  des  structures  communautaires  et  éducatives  pour l’identification  et  la  prise  en  charge  des  enfants  vulnérables  (parmi les autres, ENA/S, EAFGA) y compris ceux touchés par la LRA;
2)  mise  en  place  d’un  système  d’appui  psychosocial  et  réinsertion 
socio-économique intégré;
3)  facilitation  de  l’accès  aux  services  éducatifs,  considérés  comme 
portes  d’entré  pour  favoriser  un  environnement  protecteur  pour  les 
enfants;
4)  prévention,  protection  et  prise  en  charge  des  survivante/s  VBG. 
L’intervention  donnera  des  réponses  immédiates  à  des problématiques d’urgence et à des besoins critiques à la population la plus vulnérable dans les villes de Zemio, Obo, Djema et principaux axes  routiers  à  travers  des  points focaux  villageoises  déjà  formés dans des précédents projets.L’action renforcera donc l’accès aux services de base (promotion de l'accès  aux  mécanismes  de  protection  communautaires  et  des services  éducatifs  des  personnes  les plus  vulnérables  et  avecbesoins  spécifique),  et  la  résilience,  en  réduisant  la  violence intracommunautaire  dans  une  zone  où  la  situation  humanitaire devient de plus en plus complexe et l’intégration  des PDIs dans  les communautés hôtes de plus en plus difficile.

The main objectives of the project are; 

•	Ensure continued and regular access and availability of food and diet diversification amongst highly vulnerable male and female returnees, IDPs and host communities in Jonglei, Eastern Equatorial and Upper Nile States. 
•	Create, protect, rehabilitate and promote livelihoods by providing adequate agricultural and livelihood inputs, including cash based programming and livestock and value addition interventions to vulnerable households in Jonglei, Eastern Equatorial and Upper Nile States.

The main objective of the project is to reduce occurrence and risk of disease and death as a result of poor WASH standards by;

•	Providing sustained access to safe and adequate water, sanitation and hygiene promotion services to under-served and vulnerable populations affected by conflict, floods, disease outbreaks and acute malnutrition in Jonglei, Eastern Equatorial and Upper Nile States. 
•	Enhancing emergency WASH capacities of local communities, authorities to improve preparedness, resilience and response for the under-served vulnerable population affected by conflict, floods, disease outbreaks, and acute malnutrition in Jonglei, Eastern Equatorial and Upper Nile states.

The main objective of the project is to improve the quality of and access to mental health services in target locations by 
I)  Strengthening mental systems and structures;
II) Improving living conditions of mental health patients through Community mobilization and support

The main objectives of the project are to;
•	Risk of GBV is reduced and quality of response improved;
•	Peaceful co-existence between IDP and Host Communities strengthened;
•	GBV Coordination and participations strengthened;

The main objectives of the project are to;
•	Ensure people affected by conflict will have access to safe water;
•	Ensure people affected by conflict will have access to clean sanitation facilities;
•	People affected by conflict will have increased level of hygiene awareness, so that they are less likely to be affected by cholera and other diseases;

•	To provide NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Sudan with safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites; 
•	To transport light cargo such as medical supplies, high energy foods and information and communications technology equipment; and 
•	To provide adequate capacity for security and medical evacuations of humanitarian staff.

UNHCR activities focus on identified Protection Cluster priorities:

1. Provide immediate protection support to highly at-risk populations, including people in difficult-to-reach and unstable environments; 
2. Provide specialized support to populations suffering from abuse and violence and facilitate community-based support for families and people affected by the conflict; 
3. Engage with authorities and humanitarian partners to promote full adherence to international protection norm and humanitarian and human rights law and facilitate community-based approaches to protection.

•	To provide a safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites for  NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Mali; 
•	To transport light cargo such as medical supplies, high-energy food and information and communications technology equipment; and
•	To provide security evacuations for the humanitarian community within Mali.

1.	Support provision of basic maternal health services in newly accessible communities; 
2.	Increase access to and utilisation of family planning commodities including LARCS for women of reproductive age;
3.	Increase access to and utilisation of other SRH services such as ANC, skilled delivery services including emergency obstetric care, PNC, PAC, clinical management of rape, HCT and PMTCT and STI screening and management and;
4.	Provide an entry point for psycho-social support and GBV services

The objectives of the project are as follows;

•	Protect, advocate and monitor children's right to life and survival
•	Ensure access to life-saving services for conflict-affected girls and boys

• To provide a safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites for NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Nigeria;
• To transport life-saving cargo including medical supplies and high-energy foods;
• To perform adequate capacity for evacuations of humanitarian staff.

The objectives above are linked to WFP’s Strategic Results Framework and are examples of Strategic Objective 1: Save lives and protect livelihoods in emergencies.

The overall objective of this project is to reverse the rising mortality rate from communicable diseases, malnutrition, kala azar, unsafe child deliveries and support victims of SGBV with a focus on reaching the most vulnerable conflict affected U5 children, adolescent girls and women.  

This objective will be achieved by:
1.	Improving access and scale up responsiveness to an integrated essential primary healthcare package focusing on the major causes of mortality among U5 children (malaria, diarrhea, pneumonia and measles), SAM with medical complications, emergency HIV/AIDS and Tuberculosis, BEmONC and clinical management of SGBV;
2.	Strengthening health systems to prevent, detect and respond to epidemic prone disease outbreaks (kala azar, measles, cholera);
3.	Ensuring essential clinical health services are inclusive and implemented with dignity targeting specific needs of vulnerable populations (women and adolescent girls);
4.	Improving access to psychosocial support and mental health services for the vulnerable population, including those services related to the GBV response.

Two strategies will address the access restrictions experienced by vulnerable people. First, a strong approach of medical and SAM case finding outreaches will ensure vulnerable HHs will have access to high quality services, including immunization services, SAM services, MHSSP for survivors of SGBV. Second, through direct referrals to hospitals and specialized services, the project will ensure that patients showing severe HIV/AIDS complications and symptoms of TB will be treated, and those needing post-rape treatment will have access to required services.  CMA recognizes that insecurity creates a situation of vulnerability for women, adolescent girls and children. The project will help communities implement protection practices to reduce risk of GBV and enable women and children to have unimpeded access to health services.

The overall objective of the project is to provide a first-line response to the WASH needs of the population (IDPs, Returnees and Host Communities) in camps and outside of camps by:
- Ensuring safe, sustained, equitable access to a sufficient quantity of water to meet basic drinking, domestic and personal hygiene needs;
- Ensuring satisfactory sanitation and hygiene living conditions, with a particular focus on communities and local authorities empowerment.

Objective 1: Facilitate voluntary repatriation of Afghan refugees;

Objective 2: Ensure protection of refugees in Pakistan and improve their access to legal assistance;

Objective 3: Empower refugees, particularly children and youth (5 24 years), through education, vocational skills and livelihoods opportunities;

Objective 4: Improve access to primary health care services for targeted refugee and host communities;

Objective 5: Increase tolerance towards Afghan refugees in Pakistan, promote their social cohesion and peaceful co existence with host communities and reduce their social and economic vulnerabilities through implementation of RAHA projects.

Objectif global : Fournir et maintenir des abris et des NFI aux IDPs afin de les permettre de vivre en toute dignité et sécurité.

Objectifs spécifiques :
1.	Fournir des abris temporaires d’urgence et abris transitionnels aux personnes déplacées, aux populations retournées et aux familles hôtes vulnérables ;
2.	Maintenir et rénover les anciens abris en semi-durables /transitionnels avec l’implication de la communauté cible pour assurer une protection efficace des familles vulnérables (déplacés et familles hôtes) ;
3.	Fournir des kits NFI d’urgence aux personnes nouvellement déplacées et renouveler régulièrement certains éléments des kits en fonction des besoins.

Contribute to the reduction of mortality and morbidity rate, related to AWD/Cholera in target locations of South and Puntland regions through: 
1.	Provision of access to adequate and safe water for IDPs and host communities to reduce risk of waterborne diseases and Gender Based Violence mainly against women and girls;
2.	Provision of access to improved sanitation facilities that are culturally acceptable and provide equal access to the female and male users including people with disabilities   
3.	Supporting and instilling a culture of good hygiene practice to reduce risk of related diseases among IDPs and Host communities in target locations;

1.Fournir l'analyse constante de la situation de protection ;
2.Contribuer à répondre aux besoins urgents de personnes les plus vulnérables victimes de violences à travers une assistance holistique ;
3.Identifier et analyser les différents types de conflits intercommunautaires et ceux portant sur l'accès aux ressources afin de créer un environnement favorable de protection et de retour durable des rapatriés et des retournés ;
4.Prévenir les risques de recrutement, du trafic humain et l’engagement des filles et femmes dans les activités du sexe pour la survie à travers les activités de moyens de subsistence et de rapprochement communautaire.

L’objectif global est de restaurer et renforcer les capacités productives des ménages victimes du conflit et des inondations en phase de retour sur leurs exploitations agricoles et leur permettre ainsi de vivre dignement de l’agriculture pour la couverture de leurs besoins.
Objectif spécifique 1 : Appuyer la reprise de la production agricoles de base pour assurer la disponibilité alimentaire au niveau des ménages vulnérables par un apport d’intrants agricoles de base ;
Objectif spécifique 2 : Apporter l’assistance technique pour la diversification et l’amélioration de l’alimentation et des revenus par la promotion du maraîchage et de l’élevage à cycle court ;
Objectif spécifiques 3 : Réhabiliter les petits périmètres maraîchers (PPM) et périmètres irrigués villageois (PIV) pour accroître les capacités de production et de diversification des ressources alimentaires et monétaires.

L’objectif global est de rétablir la sécurité alimentaire des ménages d’éleveurs (pasteurs et agro pasteurs) par un appui à l’amélioration de l’accès aux intrants d’élevage, à l’eau et la promotion de pratiques d’élevage adaptées au contexte de changement climatique.
Objectif spécifique 1 : Recapitaliser les ménages vulnérables par la reconstitution du cheptel caprin ;
Objectif spécifique 2 : Assurer l’accès à l’eau par la réhabilitation des puits pastoraux dans les zones de retour des populations d’éleveurs déplacées et rapatriées ;
Objectif spécifiques 3 : Promouvoir la constitution de réserves fourragères par la réhabilitation des bourgoutières et la régénération naturelles des pâturages, l’ensemencement de pâturages artificiels, la vulgarisation de la collecte, le traitement des pailles et le stockage du foin pour l’alimentation du bétail durant les périodes de soudure.

•	To provide regular, safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites for  NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Cameroon; 
•	To transport light cargo such as medical supplies and high-energy foods; 
•	To provide adequate capacity for evacuations of humanitarian staff.

The general objective of the proposed project is women and girls affected by conflict have increased access to life saving services and protection to reduce impact and risks of GBV in Rubkona County and at IDP sites in Juba. The project outcome is that conflict affected communities are better protected from gender based violence and survivors access specialized services for GBV case management; associated outputs are: 
1- Risks and spikes in sexual violence, including conflict related sexual violence, are detected and protection mechanisms are activated to respond; 
2- GBV survivors access specialised medical, legal and psychosocial services according to their needs;
3- Communities know which GBV related services are available and how to access them and the local authorities, communities, leaders, humanitarian actors are engaged in the reduction of GBV risks in the affected communities; 
4- Local organisations’ capacity and participation in GBV emergency response and preparedness is increased. 
The project directly contributes to sectoral objective 2 as planned in the Humanitarian Response Plan for South Sudan as it contributes directly to prevention of GBV, and protection and case management of GBV survivors, ensuring services are available in IDP sites. The project is also aligned to the Beyond Bentiu Response Strategy, the South Sudan national GBV Subcluster strategy and the IRC South Sudan Strategic Action Plan.

Health status of the population improved; 
Population has optimal access to education; 
Population has sufficient basic domestic items; 
Services for persons with specific needs strengthened; 
Shelter and infrastructure established, improved and maintained

1.	Promouvoir le développement maraicher en milieu scolaire ; 
2.	Améliorer l'alimentation en milieu scolaire ; 
3.	Contribuer à l’amélioration de la sécurité alimentaire et la réduction de la pauvreté en milieu rural par la restauration des bases de la production agro-sylvo-pastorale  au profit de femmes vulnérables

The overall objective is to maintain and sustain the existing basic essential emergency health services with improved emergency referral services for communities in Duk County with a focus on women, children & IDPs/returnees.  
The specific objectives are:
1. To increase the population reached with provision of emergency primary health care services with particular focus on maternal and child health care both preventive and curative services to the most vulnerable group of the rural communities in Duk County;
2. To improve the quality of basic health services by pre-positioning of essential drugs, medical equipment & supplies to all the targeted health facilities in Duk county; 
3. To strengthen health facilities and capacity of health staffs to respond to quality emergency health services in Duk County.

The main objectives of the project are;
• Sustainable access to safe and sufficient quantity of drinking water (SPHERE standards) from newly drilled and/or rehabilitated boreholes to IDPs, host communities, returnees and children with a special focus on women and the girl child, and;
• Improved hygiene behaviour among IDPs, host communities, returnees and children with a special focus on women and the girl child
IAS is one of the founding members of Core Humanitarian Standards Alliance (CHS Alliance) 

The main objectives of the project feed into all three Cluster Objective.

DDG-DRC wish to implement an integrated Mine Action programme in specific support of Shelter and Livelihoods programme in the GCA parts of the Donbas region of eastern Ukraine (Cluster objectives 1,2 and 3). The project objective of creating a safe environment free from mines and explosive remnants of war will realise the following outcomes:

1.	Prevent injuries and save lives, for individuals still living in conflict-affected communities and for returning IDPs;
2.	Ensure the safe access for DRC-DDG Shelter and Livelihoods programmes and other humanitarian organizations and the delivery of vital humanitarian assistance.

Implementation of food and non-food based interventions to support vulnerable and affected Syrians to restore, enhance and protect livelihoods of people affected by the current crises; strengthen the resilience of people and communities; support the return of IDPs/Refugees; through:
• Provision of income generation opportunities;
• Asset creation and rehabilitation;
• Vocational Skills development;
• Support to small-medium local food production systems;
• Retail sector rehabilitation;

The results of the project are: 
(i)	Women and girls former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable women refugees/ and survivors of SGBV, have increased access to holistic ( psychosocial medical, judiciary)support ; 
(ii)	The capacity of protection actors ( police, justice ,health workers, humanitarian actors in service and pre service are reinforced to close up existing protection gaps; 
(iii)	The GBV information system including referral and counter referral is strengthened and functional at local and national levels 
(iv)	 Women and girls former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable women refugees and survivors of SGBV have increased their income to meet urgent needs and promote social cohesion.

?	To stabilize and improve food and nutrition security of the most vulnerable households; 
?	To reduce sharing within families of the expensive specialized nutrition commodities for treatment of malnutrition, meant only for malnourished members; and 
?	To improve uptake and coverage of nutrition services, improving the effectiveness of nutrition treatment.

The overall goal of the project is to protect the life, dignity and health of vulnerable population from risk of gender –based violence imposed by the armed conflict in eastern Ukraine. This goal is pursued through the following:
1) Providing GBV survivors with inclusive access to good-quality essential protection and psycho-social services and safe-space solutions as well as referral to SRH and legal counseling objectives:
2) Fostering a supportive environment at the regional/ municipal and community levels to deal with GBV. 
3) Scaling up and integration into national systems of the multi-sectoral response to GBV (including prevention, mitigation and management of GBV cases);  
Project specific objectives:
• Ensure free and non-discriminatory access to quality essential GBV services (PSS, health, legal aid etc.) based on the survivor-centered approach for all GBV survivors (boys, man, woman and adolescent girls); 
• Raised awareness of vulnerable populations of risks, rights and availability of essential GBV services;
• Increased community engagement and mobilization in support of GBV survivors;
• Improved multi-sectoral coordination of GBV response among key stakeholders, integration of GBV prevention and response into other sectors (livelihood programs) and gradual transfer of coordination responsibilities to national authorities;
• Identify durable solutions to sustain provision of essential psycho-social and safe space (shelters) services for GBV survivors based on regional/municipal authorities engagement;  

The major geographical focus of the project will be at Donetsk and Luhansk oblasts (GCA, areas along the contact line, NGCA(through project partners), whilst the focus in Kharkiv, Dnipro and Zaporizhia oblasts will be targeted at durable solutions and transition and full transfer of ownership over coordination and response to national mechanisms

Le renforcement de l’environnement de protection pour les personnes affectées et à risque, afin de réduire les risques de protection et violations des droits humains ;
L’amélioration d’un accès équitable, sûr et digne aux services de protection
La promotion des stratégies d’autonomisation et l’appropriation des mécanismes communautaires de protection avec un accent sur l’âge, le genre et la diversité par les bénéficiaires

In line with the Education Sector objectives for 2018, this project will: 
1.	Prevent vulnerable children from dropping out of school; 
2.      Provide out-of-school children with a means to continue education and re-enter formal education; 
3..	Build the capacity of schools in Syria to respond to urgent material and psycho-social needs of children through training on PSS support and provision of school kits; 
4.     Provide access to safe and appropriate environments for formal and non-formal education through school rehabilitation

