L’objectif global est de rétablir la sécurité alimentaire des ménages d’éleveurs (pasteurs et agro pasteurs) par un appui à l’amélioration de l’accès aux intrants d’élevage, à l’eau et la promotion de pratiques d’élevage adaptées au contexte de changement climatique.
Objectif spécifique 1 : Recapitaliser les ménages vulnérables, dont ceux dont la femme est chef de ménage, par la reconstitution du cheptel caprin ;
Objectif spécifique 2 : Assurer l’accès à l’eau par la réhabilitation des puits pastoraux dans les zones de retour des populations d’éleveurs déplacées et rapatriées ;
Objectif spécifiques 3 : Promouvoir la constitution de réserves fourragères par la réhabilitation des bourgoutières et la régénération naturelles des pâturages, l’ensemencement de pâturages artificiels, la vulgarisation de la collecte, le traitement des pailles et le stockage du foin pour l’alimentation du bétail durant les périodes de soudure.

Contribuer à la réduction de la mortalité et morbidité liées à la malnutrition chez les populations les plus à risque. De façon spécifique, il s’agira de :
1. Fournir des suppléments nutritionnels aux enfants de 6-23 mois, femmes enceintes et allaitantes dans les zones vulnérables afin de prévenir la détérioration de leur état nutritionnel ;
2. Assurer une prise en charge adéquate aux enfants de 6-59 mois et femmes enceintes/allaitantes malnutris modérés.
3. Fournir du cash aux femmes pour incitations à la fréquentation des centres de santé ;
4. Fournir une assistance aux accompagnants des enfants malnutris sévères avec complications hospitalisés dans les URENI ;
5. Renforcer le système de dépistage/référencement des cas de malnutrition vers les structures de santé, le suivi/évaluation des activités de nutrition
6. Renforcer les capacités des structures de santé pour assurer la supervision et la coordination des interventions de nutrition

Réduire les risques de mortalité et de morbidité liées à la malnutrition aigüe sévère chez les enfants de moins de 5 ans, au Mali et spécifiquement :
- Prendre en charge les enfants souffrant de la malnutrition aigüe sévère avec ou sans complications médicales ;
- Assurer la surveillance nutritionnelle et le dépistage des cas de malnutrition aigüe ; 
- Renforcer les capacités des structures de santé et des communautés pour une prise en charge plus durable des cas de Malnutris aigus ;
- Soutenir les activités de prévention contre la malnutrition chez les enfants de moins de 5 ans
-Renforcer la coordination à tous les niveaux dans le domaine de la lutte contre la malnutrition

To provide Humanitarian and development actors, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Sudan with safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites; 
•To transport light cargo, namely medical supplies and information communications technology equipment; and 
•To respond in a timely manner to the security and medical evacuation needs of humanitarian and development actors, as/when required.

• To provide non-governmental organizations (NGOs), United Nations (UN) agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Nigeria with safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites;
• To transport light cargo such as medical supplies, high energy foods, and information and communications technology (ICT) equipment; and
• To provide adequate capacity for evacuations of humanitarian staff.

The objective of the Project is to provide regular, safe, effective and efficient air service to:
-  facilitate access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites for  humanitarian actors     (NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Cameroon); 
- transport light cargo including medical supplies and high energy foods; 
- provide adequate capacity for medical evacuations of humanitarian staff

The project aims to provide holistic support to survivors of gender-based violence in four localities (Mayo Sava, Mayo Tsanaga, Diamare, and Logone et Chari) while strengthening the capacity of relevant national actors for sustainability with effective coordination. There are four outputs: 
(i) Women and girls including former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable women refugees/and survivors of GBV have increased access to holistic (psycho-social, psychiatric and mental, medical, legal and economic) support;
(ii) The capacity of protection actors (police, justice, health, humanitarian actors) is strengthened to meet the needs of GBV survivors;
(iii) Women and girls including former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable woman refugees and GBV survivors have increased their income to meet immediate needs while maintaining social cohesion;
(iv) The GBV coordination is strengthened and functional at local and regional levels.

Crisis affected girls and boys (3-18 years) have access to safe, protective and inclusive learning environments including protective services and referral pathways through schools:

World Relief has set out to achieve the following objectives from the humanitarian interventions in the affected communities through access to safe, protective, inclusive and quality education, including recreation, life-skills messages, psychosocial support and emergency school feeding for the improved learning opportunities to children in Koch, Guit (Unity State) and Fangak County (Jonglei State) and Ibba, Mvolo (Western Equatoria); 
The planned intervention aims at providing not only safe, inclusive and protective learning spaces (during establishment /setting /rehabilitation, environmentally friendly and local building materials will be used where possible, to minimize environmental impact) with gender segregated WASH facilities and teaching and recreational materials. The provision of emergency school feeding through school meals and transfers of food resources to entire families conditional upon school enrollment and regular attendance of children and also build capacities of education actors (teachers) and communities (PTA) in protecting right of children for education in this humanitarian crisis and supporting recruited volunteer teachers through incentives, according to recent national and cluster guidelines and standards. The project will give special attention to girls’ education as their enrollment rate is very low and dropout rate is very high in the country. This vulnerable segment of the society will be primary targets of the project as they are victims of harmful traditional and cultural practices.

In line with the Protection sector overall objectives for 2018, this project will: 
1.	Improve the protection of the crisis affected population, through community based and individually targeted protection interventions; 
2.	Strengthen the capacity of duty bearers at community level to assess, analyze, prevent and address protection risks and needs; 
3.	Improve the access to child protection for boys and girls in need, in line with the Child Protection Minimum Standards in Humanitarian Action.

1.Populations affected by disasters in target areas meet their basic food and nutrition needs during and after crises;

2.Food-insecure people affected by long-term conflict and/or displacement are able to meet their basic food and nutrition needs and increase their self-reliance throughout the year;

3.Food-insecure residents in targeted areas have sustainably improved nutrition by 2021

Objective 1: Monitor and advocate for access to territory, prevention of refoulement and promotion of and respect for refugees’ rights.
Objective 2: Enhance registration of all refugee women and men, girls and boys and facilitate their access to documentation                                                                       
Objective 3: Promote a community-based approach to the response and provide protection services to persons at heightened risk;
Objective 4: Support system strengthening and social cohesion within refugee and host communities;

-	Provide safe, appropriate emergency shelter and critical life-saving non-food items to newly displaced or highly vulnerable populations in secondary displacement;
-	Improve the living conditions of highly vulnerable IDPs in protracted displacement and host communities through shelter and non-food item assistance in accordance with Cluster minimum standards;
-	Protect highly vulnerable families from climatic conditions through timely seasonal shelter and non-food item assistance in accordance with Cluster technical guidelines;
-	Support highly vulnerable families in the process of return, and non-displaced families, through shelter and non-food item assistance in accordance with Cluster minimum standards.

To provide emergency and sustainable WaSH support to returnees and IDPs in Nineveh and Salah Al Din Governorates of Iraq according to cluster objectives: 
- Support protracted IDPs in water, sanitation services along with promoting hygienic environment ;
- Provide WaSH emergency services to highly vulnerable and newly displaced populations;
- Facilitate, through community and local authorities involvement, cost effective and equitable WaSH services;

Through this intervention, ACTED will contribute to the WASH Cluster’s objectives 1, 2, and 3:
1.	Restore, improve standards, and sustain water and sanitation services and provide an enabling environment for good hygiene practices to vulnerable individuals in protracted displacement and return settings;
2.	Provide safe and accessible emergency water and sanitation services and support good hygiene practices for highly vulnerable populations in and out of camps, new displacement and at risk areas after shocks;
3.	Facilitate the establishment of sustainable, cost effective, and equitable water and sanitation services including community-focused hygiene promotion, and facilitate take over of care and maintenance to communities and local authorities;

The project it is planned to complement ACTED’s multi-sectoral interventions in areas with a high prevalence of new returnees, vulnerable non-displaced families and households in protracted displacement. ACTED’s strategy for 2018 is based on the application of an area-based approach that allows to simultaneously address the different needs of vulnerable population groups in conflict-affected areas. 
For example, ACTED’s programming aims at re-establishing public service provision through Quick Impact Projects and the rehabilitation of communal WASH infrastructures; favouring returns through emergency repairs on private housing and distribution of core NFIs lost or damaged during displacement and looting; supporting IDPs willing to return and newly displaced households with multi-purpose cash assistance; creating income-generating opportunities through livelihoods interventions; and addressing urgent gaps in child protection through community-based approaches. 
Additionally, ACTED will provide in-camp and off-camp CCCM support, including the establishment of Urban Displacement Resource Centres in priority urban return areas.

Appuyer le Dispositif National de Prévention et de Gestion des Crises alimentaires, nutritionnelles et pastorales en lui apportant des moyens conséquents pour l’accomplissement de sa mission et stimuler un suivi plus régulier et plus élargi des indicateurs de la sécurité alimentaire et d’alerte précoce au Tchad. L'appui permet de soutenir les activités suivantes : 
i) Enquête nationale de le sécurité alimentaire (ENSA) ; 
ii) Etude annuelle des marchés ;
iii) Enquêtes exigées par des événements inattendus (EFSA) ; 
iv) Enquêtes à distance (mVAM) ;
v) Evaluations sommaires (campagne, inondations, zones à risque, etc…) ; 
vi) Suivi des campagnes agricoles par l’activation du groupe de travail pluridisciplinaire (GTP) ; 
vii) Oorganisation des sessions du cadre harmonisé (CH) ; 
viii) Activités de African Risk Capacity (ARC) ;
ix) Collecte, analyse et remontée des informations par les CRA, CDA et CLA ; et
x) Etudes d’économie des ménages (HEA)

