The objective of this project is to support mine-affected communities in eastern Ukraine by:
 - protecting civilian lives and facilitating the safe resettlement of internally displaced persons (IDPs); 
 - improving socioeconomic opportunities of mine affected communities by allowing contaminated land to be brought back into 
   productive use;
 - promoting the rehabilitation of items of critical infrastructure damaged or rendered inaccessible as a result of mines.

""Objective 1: Provide life-saving and life-sustaining humanitarian health assistance with an emphasis on those most at risk and in need, through:
• Provision of the Essential Package of Primary Health Services (including trauma, child and reproductive health, gender-based violence, integrated nutrition, management of non-communicable diseases, mental health and psychosocial support, people living with disabilities, and referral services for continuity of care); 
• Availability of essential medicines, medical supplies, equipment and provision of outreach services where needed;
• Implementing the Expanded Programme on Immunization (EPI), where available, to enhance vaccination coverage up to required levels.

Objective 2: Strengthen health sector coordination and health information systems to improve the effectiveness of life-saving health response for people in need, with an emphasis on enhancing protection and increasing access for health, by:
• Improving communication between health actors sharing area presence, and coordination of local and regional care networks to avoid duplication of services and facilitate referrals;

Objective 3: Improve access to basic services and livelihoods by supporting community resilience, institutional and response capacity, through:
• Training, retaining and increasing the capacity of health care providers and community health workers 
• Strengthening partnerships and capacity building with NNGOs;
• Rehabilitation and re-enforcement of damaged health facilities, including physical, human resources, equipment/supplies to provide safe and secure environments for health service delivery
• Maintenance and upgrading of the epidemiological surveillance, detection and response system (EWARN)

1. Garantir aux filles et aux garçons en âge scolaire (3-16 ans) affectés par la situation sociopolitique et économique ainsi que les catastrophes naturelles, un accès à une éducation de qualité adaptée à leurs besoins socio-émotionnels, dans un environnement sain, protecteur et non discriminatoire ;

2. Renforcer les capacités des structures éducatives dans la prévention et la gestion des situations d'urgence et des risques de catastrophes en tenant compte des spécificités de genre et d'âge ;

3. Renforcer les mécanismes de plaidoyer, de suivi/évaluation et de coordination des acteurs et des interventions pour faire face aux situations d'urgence dans le domaine de l’Education.

The overall objective of the project is to contribute to the sustainable improvement of food security and nutritional status for vulnerable households in Nara district, Mali. This will be achieved through the achievement of two specific objectives:
Specific objective 1: Strengthen the capacity of vulnerable households to meet their basic food and nutritional needs, avoid negative coping strategies, and prevent acute malnutrition in children under five and pregnant and lactating women;
Specific objective 2: Strengthen coverage of malnutrition prevention services for children under five in vulnerable food-insecure households.

Protection Sector 5: Increased and more equitable access for boys and girls to quality protection in targeted locations in line with the Child Protection Minimum Standards in Humanitarian Action (main objective);  
Protection Sector  3: Survivors have access to quality specialized GBV services and measures are in place to prevent and reduce risks of GBV;
Protection Sector 1: The protection of the population affected by the crisis is improved through community-based and individually targeted protection interventions.

L’objectif global est de restaurer et renforcer les capacités productives des ménages victimes, dont les femmes vulnérables chefs de ménage, du conflit et des inondations en phase de retour sur leurs exploitations agricoles et leur permettre ainsi de vivre dignement de l’agriculture pour la couverture de leurs besoins.
Objectif spécifique 1 : Appuyer la reprise de la production agricoles de base pour assurer la disponibilité alimentaire au niveau des ménages vulnérables par un apport d’intrants agricoles de base ;
Objectif spécifique 2 : Apporter l’assistance technique pour la diversification et l’amélioration de l’alimentation et des revenus par la promotion du maraîchage et de l’élevage à cycle court en faveur principalement des ménages dirigés par des femmes ;
Objectif spécifiques 3 : Réhabiliter les petits périmètres maraîchers (PPM) et périmètres irrigués villageois (PIV) pour accroître les capacités de production et de diversification des ressources alimentaires et monétaires.

The project aims to provide holistic support to survivors of gender-based violence in Minawao refugee camp and surrounding host communities while strengthening the capacity of relevant national actors for sustainability with effective coordination. There are four outputs: 
(i) Women and girls including former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable women refugees/and survivors of GBV have increased access to holistic (psycho-social, psychiatric and mental, medical, legal and economic) support;
(ii) The capacity of protection actors (police, justice, health, humanitarian actors) is strengthened to meet the needs of GBV survivors;
(iii) Women and girls including former hostages of Boko Haram, vulnerable woman refugees and GBV survivors have increased their income to meet immediate needs while maintaining social cohesion;
(iv) The GBV coordination is strengthened and functional at local and regional levels.

L’objectif global est d’apporter un soutien à la coordination effective des activités d’urgence et de réhabilitation agricole et de promouvoir une meilleure prise en compte des besoins des groupes spécifiquement vulnérables.
Objectif spécifique 1 : Assurer la coordination inclusive des acteurs humanitaires intervenant dans le domaine de la sécurité alimentaire par un meilleur partage des informations et une planification conjointe de la réponse ;
Objectif spécifique 2 : Assurer que les besoins des groupes spécifiquement vulnérables sont évalués et intégrés dans les plans de réponse humanitaire et national par une meilleure définition des critères de ciblage ;
Objectif spécifique 3 : Apporter une assistance technique pour la prévention et la lutte intégré contre les chenilles légionnaires.

The objective of this project to ensure that displaced individuals and households have access to appropriate assistance inside and outside of formal camp settings. A particular focus will be on identifiying the most vulnerable households within the displaced populations, and ensuring that specialized assistance is provided, as well as promoting conditions that work towards durable solutions. If necessary, camp consolidation and closure processes will be implemented, including the capacity building of relevant actors. This project will focus on displaced populations within Ninewa, Salah Al-Din, and Anbar primarily.

The project is in line with all four cluster objectives: 
Objective 1. Enable a safe and dignified livable environment for displaced people in formal and informal settlements across Iraq; 
Objective 2. Support informed, safe, and voluntary return processes for highly vulnerable IDPs; 
Objective 3. Empower returnee, non-displaced, host families and IDP in return areas through community participation and coordination; 
Objective 4. Facilitate and advocate for a safe and dignified livable environment for newly displaced people across Iraq.

Apres l’instabilité politique de 2012 qui a déstabilisé plusieurs institutions nationales, le Mali s’efforce de refaire les bases d’un secteur AEPHA fonctionnel. Quelques actions entreprises dans ce sens sont : revues des politiques nationales de l’eau et l’assainissement en 2006 et 2009 au regard de ces politiques qui n’étaient pas alignées aux ODD de 2015 que le mali a signé. Mais récemment les plans et stratégies du secteur au niveau national ont permis de clarifier la vision et ses liens avec les ODD.  Il y a des insuffisances à combler dans les politiques. Le financement de l’Etat au secteur AEPHA reste faible avec un taux de 1.57% en 2016. Ce taux n’a pas connu d’augmentation pendant la période des OMD au regard de la forte dépendance continue du secteur vis-à-vis des partenaires au développement qui apportent environ 90% pour la fourniture des services et certaines questions transversales.  Du point de vue régional, le cercle de Gao fait partir des 27 cercles  prioritaires identifiés les plus affectés par la combinaison des catastrophes d’origine naturelle (Inondations et Sécheresse) et humaine (Conflits et attaques) et de la sévérité du manque d’accès à l’eau au titre de la réponse humanitaire  2019 au Mali.
Selon WaSH HNO 2019, la fréquence et l’intensité des évènements de sècheresse et d’inondations engendrent de nouveaux besoins humanitaires car au Mali, les vulnérabilités ne s’ajoutent pas mais elles se multiplient, notamment dans les régions du Nord et du Centre affectées par le conflit.  Les déplacements et les besoins pastoraux accentuent la pression autour des ressources en eau et génèrent des tensions intercommunautaires. La malnutrition est liée à l'accès à l'eau potable, l'hygiène et l'assainissement et plus de la moitié des cas de malnutrition est associée aux maladies diarrhéiques.  
Les Groupes vulnérables les plus exposés aux catastrophes demeurent les agriculteurs, les éleveurs, les femmes et les enfants. Ce Projet de par les activités à implémenter intègre à la fois les Secteurs WaSH, Nutrition, Protection et le Partenariat avec une ONG locale. En plus des activités EHA plus amplement détaillées, des activités de lutte contre la malnutrition seront également mises en œuvre dont notamment:
•Délivrance du paquet minimum WASH in NUT à 1000 couples/enfants dans le cadre de la prise en charge nutritionnelle ;
•Intégration des femmes à des postes clés dans les Comités de gestion ;
•Mise en place d’un cadre de partenariat avec l’ONG locale FAABA qui met en œuvre les activités d’intermédiation sociale et de promotion de l’hygiène ;
•Collaboration avec les autorités administratives, la tutelle technique en vue de la pérennisation des ouvrages etc . Dans l’optique d’assurer la stratégie de sortie du Projet, non seulement un accent particulier sera mis sur l’appropriation par une auto gestion des infrastructures hydrauliques et sanitaires construites/réhabilitées. En outre, Help travaille déjà en partenariat dans le cadre de la Promotion de l’Hygiène avec l’ONG locale FAABA. Pour ce faire, le Projet implémentera les activités suivantes :
-Redynamisation ou renforcement des capacités des Comités de Gestion (CGPE, CVS et CSS et hygiénistes) qui seront dotés de kits d’hygiène de maintenance et d’entretien : cela octroiera aux Comités toutes les potentialités pour une gestion plus efficace et efficiente des ouvrages qui leurs seront confiés ;
-La dotation en kits outils matériels et équipements, ainsi que le renforcement des capacités des autorités locales (Mairies, Préfecture et sous-préfecture) et services techniques (DRH, DRACPN, CAP, DRS, CSRéf) : cette implication des autorités améliorera davantage l’appropriation et la bonne gestion des infrastructures qui leurs seront remises.

Le présent projet d’Appui à l’Amélioration de la Situation Nutritionnelle pour les populations vulnérables de la Région de Tombouctou s’inscrit dans le cadre de la stratégie d’Action Contre la Faim de réponse aux urgences nutritionnelles au Nord du Mali. 
Le projet cible les enfants souffrant de Malnutrition Aigüe Sévère (MAS), les femmes en âge de procréer dont les Femmes Enceintes et Allaitantes (FEFA) dans les 9 Unités de Réhabilitation Nutritionnelle en Ambulatoire pour les malnutris Sévères (URENAS) des structures sanitaires fonctionnelles et 3 non fonctionnelles de 6 communes du District Sanitaire de Tombouctou (Salam, Ber, Alafia, Lafia, Tombouctou, et Bourem-Inaly).
Il vise à contribuer à la réduction de la prévalence de la malnutrition aigüe sévère et la mortalité liée à la malnutrition aigüe sévère dans la Région de Tombouctou (impact), en se fixant les objectifs suivants : 
•	Assurer une prise en charge nutritionnelle de qualité des enfants de moins de 5 ans et des femmes enceintes et allaitantes (FEFA) ; 
•	Améliorer les pratiques d’Alimentation du Nourrisson et du Jeune Enfant (ANJE) dans la communauté dans l’ensemble des aires de santé ciblées ; 
•	Améliorer la Gouvernance locale en faveur de la prise en charge de la malnutrition aigüe

Since the start of the conflict in December 2013, IOM has been providing lifesaving humanitarian assistance to Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) and host communities in conflict affected locations in South Sudan. The proposed project will build on IOMs significant experience in the provision of health and mental health and psychosocial support (MHPSS) services and will continue to support rapid response mechanisms, which provide lifesaving health services to conflict-affected populations in hard-to-access areas.

In 2019, IOM will aim to achieve the below four health cluster specific objectives: 

1 - Improve access and scale-up responsiveness to integrated essential healthcare needs of the vulnerable populations focusing on major causes of morbidity and mortality;
2 - Prevent, detect and respond to epidemic prone disease outbreaks;
3 - Increase access to essential clinical health services among vulnerable populations targeting SGBV, NCDs, SAM with Medical Complication;
4 - Improve resilience among vulnerable populations by increasing access to mental health and psychosocial support services.

Through static and rapid response teams, IOM will continue to provide essential preventative and curative primary health care (PHC) services in Bentiu, Malakal and Wau protection of civilian (PoC) sites, and other locations across South Sudan, targeting a total of 150,000 beneficiaries, of which 78,786 will be reached through mobile rapid response teams, and 71,214 will be reached through IOM's static PHCU facilities.

Health services will be provided in line with the Health Cluster’s essential health care package for PHCUs and Mobile Teams.

In 2019 REACH will continue to provide Information Management support to the CCCM cluster as well as its collaboration with other relevant CCCM stakeholders. 
With the objectives to 1) address information gaps on IDPs living in formal camps and informal sites, 2) improve planning and monitoring of humanitarian assistance and 3) facilitate risk reduction and maintenance, in and out of camp settings, REACH will conduct: Camp profiling, Risk Assessment Site Priority (RASP), and Intentions Surveys in formal camps and informal sites, Area Based Assessment (ABA) in key areas of return across Iraq.
Under these activities, the followings outputs may be developed, depending on need:
-	IDP Camp Directory; 
-	IDP Camp Profiling Comparative report;
-	Rapid Assessment Site Profile Subnational snapshots;
-	Intensions Key findings snapshots (English and Arabic) – for informal and formal sites;
-	Intensions Comparative report/storyboard;
-	ABA Area profile; 
-	ABA Maps;
-	Support to the CCCM Cluster in the form of a part time dedicated REACH Assessment Officer;
-	Support to the CRC Steering Committee and CCCM Cluster in the form of a dedicated staff member, who will assist in developing the connection between the data collected through the ABAs, the 5ws and how to create a localised, granular area-based approach in the CRCs.

It is expected that this programming will target around 52,218 IDPs living in formal sites (17,863 persons) and in camps (29,281) as well as host communities, returnees, and those never displaced all living within the catchment areas of the CRCs (5,074). As per the gender disaggregation, the project will aim at targeting equally women and men where possible.  Due to the nature of REACH activities which are focused on informing the national humanitarian community and coordination structures it is expected that the intervention will directly benefit the humanitarian community at large, thanks to the production of outputs which will be available to all clusters and partners. Internally displaced persons living in informal sites will indirectly benefit from improved informal site monitoring and referral system while IDPs living in in-camp locations will indirectly benefit from improved camp life cycle management. Moreover, those living within the catchment areas of the CRCs will indirectly benefit from programming planned against the area-based assessments.

UNHAS started operation in Cameroon in November 2015, following a request from the Humanitarian Coordinator as the provision of assistance by aid organizations was hampered by vast distances, insecurity and poor road infrastructure. In the years that followed, the security situation further deteriorated and made access to northern parts of the country, particularly the Far North extremely difficult. The introduction of UNHAS flights has therefore been vital in enabling humanitarian organizations to scale up their response and reach more people in need. Since its inception, UNHAS transported 20,725 passengers, 62 MT of light cargo and served more than 50 organizations.

For 2019, the Global Humanitarian Overview (GHO) identified 4.3 million people in Cameroon in need of humanitarian assistance (a 30% increase compared to 2018), majority of whom being women and children. In order to support the planned humanitarian response and enable access to the populations in need and project implementation sites, UNHAS aims: 
1.	To provide NGOs, UN agencies, donor organizations and diplomatic missions in Cameroon with safe, effective and efficient access to beneficiaries and project implementation sites;
2.	To transport light cargo, such as medical supplies, information and communication technology (ICT) equipment;
3.	To provide adequate capacity for medical and security evacuations of humanitarian staff.

This project aims to support the CCCM response to the immediate needs of the most vulnerable displaced populations in the northwest of Syria by providing multi-sectoral assistance in camps and improving the basic infrastructure and shelter conditions in IDP sites through: 
i)	Maintaining and managing three reception centers in A'zaz, Al-Bab, and Mezenaz, Aleppo;
ii)	Maintaining and managing the planned camp in Mareet Elkhwan, Idleb;
iii)	Providing 17,200 family tents in 88 IDP sites with tent insulation and ground insulation support, including replacement of 5,400 family tents; 
iv)	Ensuring 7,000 family tents are available to members of the CCCM cluster for the contingency stock; and
v)	Improving site infrastructure and facilities of 40 informal settlements. 

The project will also contribute to improving the management quality, accountability, and communal coping mechanisms of IDP sites through:
vi)	Enhancing camp management capacity of site managers of 40 informal settlements  through the provision of site management support;
vii)	Enhancing camp management capacity of NGOs through the provision of CCCM training; and
viii)	Enhancing the communal capacity of 40 informal settlements to address fire risk through the provision of fire safety measures and fire safety and first aid training.

Finally, the project also aims to inform the CCCM response of cluster members through:
ix) Collecting information about IDP displacements and IDP site profiles, including multi-sectoral gap analysis, and reporting to cluster members.

