The project aims to provide urgently needed WASH assistance to affected by clashes and displacement and vulnerable population in Libya including detained or stranded migrants and refugees in detention centers and urban areas, in line with two WASH strategic objectives focusing on the most vulnerable and affected population. Approximately 5000 migrants and 4000 affected Libyan families (Provision of WASH NFIs only) The proposed interventions include: 
1.	Rehabilitation of WASH facilities; 
2.	Provision of essential hygiene items to Libyans who have been affected by clashes and displacement and non-Libyans, including migrants and refugees at the DCs; 
3.	Awareness raising on hygiene practices. 
Through those activities implemented in urban settings, IOM will also address the needs of a people requiring assistance within affected Libyan communities, as is practically not feasible to separate potential beneficiaries. In addition, IOM will abide by the division of labour agreed with other humanitarian stakeholders and approved by the sector coordinator in terms of locations for implementation, to avoid potential overlapping and encourage synergies.  There shall be effort to enhance the resilience of local communities through awareness and knowledge sharing activities alongside the provision of material supports such as instillation of pumps and expansion of water networks to improve the overall WASH conditions.

Dans le but d’assurer l’accès à une éducation inclusive et pertinente de qualité dans un environnement d’apprentissage sûr et protecteur pour toutes les filles et tous les garçons de 3 à 18 ans affectés par une catastrophe naturelle ou par un conflit ainsi qu’en situation de vulnérabilité en, le PAM planifie de prendre en charge à travers un appui alimentaire 70 000 élèves dans les écoles touchées par crise au niveau de la région de Diffa, Maradi, Tillabéry et Tahoua et Agadez ; 
L’alimentation scolaire est un outil indispensable qui intervient pour favoriser l’inscription et le maintien des enfants à l’école quel que soit les problématiques qui impactent négativement   le secteur de l’éducation et cela est valable pour une population en mouvement ou pas.
Dans la plupart de cas, elle constitue la porte d’entrée pour apporter toutes autre réponse à l’éducation en période d’urgence.
Elle présente de objectifs multiples : 
•	Permettre l’inscription et le maintien des élèves à l’écoles et offre une plateforme pour dérouler toutes les autres interventions à l’écoles ; 
•	Améliore la nutrition des élèves ; 
•	Apporte un filet de sécurité aux ménages et accroit leur capacité à supporter le choc ; 
Amélioration de la qualité de l'éducation et l'appropriation de la communauté et un meilleur redressement ; 
 Les activités à réaliser sont les suivantes : 
•	Appui en alimentation scolaire au niveau des écoles rurales à travers un repas chaud pour les élèves des écoles ;
•	Activités complémentaires autour des cantines au besoin ;
•	Renforcement des capacités ;
•	Distribution des articles non alimentaire et des documents de suivi pour suivre le progrès de l’activité ;
Les Cibles : 
•	Les élèves 
•	Le CGDES 
La redevabilité :
La stratégie de mise en œuvre repose sur la participation communautaire et l’implication de la communauté :
•	La communauté est impliquée sur la gestion des vivres, 
•	Elle est formée sur la ration journalières et participe au prélèvement des vivres ; 
•	Désignent les femmes qui préparent pour les enfants 
•	Mobilise les parents pour l’apport en bois et eau de cuisine 
Les intervenants : ils interviennent dans le ciblage, la planification, la mise en œuvre et le suivi de l’activités :
•	Le Ministère de l’éducation primaire à travers la direction de la participation communautaire 
•	La DREP, les coordonnateurs des cantines scolaires 
•	Les inspections Départementales de l’éducation ;
•	Les ONGS 
•	La communauté 

Les indicateurs : 
•	Nombre de repas fournis ; 
•	Jour moyen d’alimentation par mois ;
•	Ration fille /garçons 
•	Le taux de retention 

La protection transversale :
 Des principes de protection seront incorporé dans tous le processus de mise en œuvre pour la promotion d’un accès significatif, sécuritaire et digne des personnes affectées par les crises humanitaires aux activités de réponses humanitaires. En avant de mettre en œuvre l’opération, l’analyse de risques auquel est confronté les bénéficaires sera effectué en lien avec les grandes étapes de mises en œuvre : à titre d’exemple, les livraisons seront faite de telle manière qu’il y’aura pas beaucoup des vivres sur le terrain afin de ne pas exposer les bénéficaires .

This project aims to conduct contractor-led immediate stabilisation of structurally damaged residential buildings that are already evacuated, and includes those which still present an immediate threat to life at community level through risk of collapse of architectural (balconies, rooves, NON-LOAD-BEARING WALLS) or load-bearing (foundations, walls, columns) elements in Beirut Municipality. Targetting 100 buildings (or around 9% of all Level 3 damaged buildings as per the Shelter sector's appeal needs calculations), the flash appeal scope over 3 months is seen as part of a longer 18 month scope for structural repairs. The proposed work builds on UN-Habitat's experience in repairing and rehabilitating structurally unsound heritage buildings accommodating vulnerable households in Saida and Tripoli in 2019. The current financial proposal is for immediate works COSTING 20% of the total structural repair cost. THE PER BUILDING COST OVER THE 3 MONTH FLASH APPEAL PERIOD IS $18,000, NOTING THAT EACH BUILDING IN THE TARGET AREAS (MAR MIKHAEL, KARANTINA) ACCOMMODATE APPROXIMATELY 8 HOUSEHOLDS [IE 100 BUILDINGS WITH 8 HH PER BUILDING = 800HH;  4 INDIVIDUALS PER HH = 3200 INDIVIDUALS], and that these areas contain concentrations of heritage structures which are both more expensive to address and more likely to have suffered sever damage. RESOURCE mobilisation for the remaining 80% IS underway. DONOR DISCUSSIONS ON a parallel initiative to undertake minor repairs in households in lightly damaged buildings (100 units) amounting to $0.128M  are at ADVANCED STAGES.

For the structurally unsound  buildings, the scope includes modern and heritage buildings in reflection of the building mix of the target area of Mar Mikhael & Karantina AS two of the most affected areas. In-house architectural and engineering expertise on structural works for heritage buildings will be utilised as needed. The project is geographically aligned to a blast-related CERF-funded ($800k) project UN-Habitat has already commenced with implementing partner PCPM on conditional cash for rent in Mar Mikhael (primary area) and Karantina (secondary area, anticipated gapfilling in soft collaboration with NRC); operational complementarities to meet the cash for rent needs of households evacuated from unsound buildings  will be addressed directly  through the CERF project and if necessary topped up through referrals. For the minor repairs, it is anticipated that buildings at some distance from the immediate blast zone will be targetted, in coordination with the Shelter sector,

Targetting of buildings will be based on
(1) Beirut Municipality Rapid Damage Assessment (RDA) survey of all buildings (which has been facilitated by UN-Habitat); 
(2) Field and phone survey of  occupiers of buildings surveyed (undertaken by UN-Habitat) in RDA that are evacuated or are to be evacuated;
(3) Emerging COMPREHENSIVE AND detailed assessments of damaged buildings by Order of Architects & Engineers IN BEIRUT MUNICIPALITY; 
(4) Household-level multisectoral needs analysis data from LRC.

The project will capitalise on UN-Habitat's links with the Order of Architects & Engineers as well as its staff presence currently embedded in Beirut Municipality Emergency Cell to engage in capacity-building on the shelter response to structural unsoundness, specifically at the levels of technical surveying and information management, technical works in the field and HLP awareness-raising to support the housing rights of evacuees among related issues. The HLP training will integrate gender sensitisation, reflecting the Shelter sector's prioritisation of female-headed households in particular.

The project intends to provide an integrated response to better alleviate critical health and WASH needs of populations affected by the conflict in Eastern Ukraine. First aid assistance and essential WASH services will be provided on EECPs. Population’s access to quality healthcare will be improved through the following:
-	Decrease of associated costs (voucher for free access to drugs/medicines);
-	Fuel for transportation provided to health facilities;
-	Improve quality of care by provision of essential medical supplies;
-	Develop health staff education/skills through supportive supervision and capacity building (including MHPSS);
-	Improve health education of local population; 
-	Improvement of WASH-in-Health conditions in HFs.
Morbidity linked to poor water supply systems will be decreased through repairs/rehabilitation of water supply networks.

The project intends to multiply the effects of this combined WASH and Health intervention by increasing the geographical consistency between all its components and working with an integrated approach, based on the needs. The project sites will be in the 0-5km (for GCA and NGCA) and 5-20km (GCA) zones, where needs are critical, but actual referral pathways will be taken into consideration when selecting SHFs to implement the voucher program.  

Lastly, the project seeks to both answer emergency needs (by supporting essential services at EECPs, primary healthcare in health facilities and by restoring access to water supply) and support middle/long term solutions. This is to ensure communities and stakeholders have sufficient capacities to cope with the effects of the conflict (capacity building of health staff including when it comes to the health reform, MHPSS, WASH-in-Health, etc…). The activities will be The described needs were already partially covered in 2019, however continuous activities like EECPs intervention and vouchers distribution will have to be re-funded in 2020, in order to allow sufficient time to alleviate the consequences of the protracted conflict and allow communities to build their own resilience mechanisms.  

In NGCA, PUI will work with Donbass Development Center, a local NGO that has been partnering with PUI for more than 4 years. DDC has excellent knowledge of the difficulties in the targeted areas and solid capacities to implement the e-voucher program. Additionally, this will facilitate the acceptance of the project by the de-facto authorities, who are often reluctant to let INGOs operate directly.

